{"t":1792063616,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"b9a1df4e-d1a9-446b-856c-306ab38e2e9c","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-15T07:21:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 150721\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n321 AM EDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nRERs sent for record warm lows, in addition to record highs,\nfrom yesterday.  \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Strong cold front to bring threat of severe weather and \n  gusty winds Friday, then much colder air surges in.\n\n- 2) Frost and freezing temperatures possible this weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong cold front to bring threat of severe \nweather and gusty winds Friday, then much colder air surges in.\n\nA cold front directly over our area early this morning is going to \nvery slow push south through tonight. Expect widespread clouds and \npassing showers, though most of the shower activity is likely to be \non the light side. Temperatures are going to steadily drop this \nmorning and remain in the 60s this afternoon, then drop to the 50s \ntonight. Surface high to our north slides offshore this evening \nwhich is going to cause winds over the Mid-Atlantic to veer \nsoutherly tonight. The frontal surface mostly washes out by Friday \nmorning as an area of low pressure moves from the Great Lakes into \nOntario. \n\nWarm conditions return Friday as sunshine and southwest winds push \nhighs back up to the low to mid 80s. Models continue to slow the \narrival of our next cold front. The leading edge of the front, with \nits associated line of showers and thunderstorms, is now progged to \ncross the area Friday late afternoon to evening. The later arrival \nalso allows for more instability to develop, which will act to fuel \nstronger thunderstorms. Sufficient shear should support an organized \nlike of convection, with embedded severe thunderstorms. While \ndamaging wind gusts are going to be the primary threat, a quick QLCS \ntornado spin-up is certainly possible. SPC has added a Slight Risk \nacross the northern Shenandoah Valley to northern MD, with a \nMarginal Risk covering the rest of the forecast area. \n\nThis line of thunderstorms pushes east of the area late Friday \nevening, but the main upper trough lags far behind. The system \nbecomes more anafrontal Friday night into Saturday morning, with \noverrunning showers and breezy conditions. The positively tilted \nupper trough finally swings through Saturday afternoon, with \nconditions drying out from northwest to southeast.\n\nDue to the less amplified nature of the upper trough we probably \ndon't reach Wind Advisory criteria, even in the mountains. Still, \ngusty conditions with peak gusts around 30-40 mph likely Saturday \nafternoon. The biggest story is going to be the much colder airmass \nthat settles over the region. Highs Saturday only reach the mid to \nupper 50s as strong cold advection persists behind the cold front.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Frost and freezing temperatures possible this\nweekend.\n\nA sharp transition to cold weather is expected behind a strong cold \nfront this weekend. This dry airmass in combination with clearing \nskies and weakening winds Saturday night creates an ideal \nenvironment for efficient radiational cooling conditions throughout \nthe area. Overnight temperatures could drop to near or below \nfreezing west of the Blue Ridge on Saturday and Sunday night. Most \nareas across our region have opportunities for frost to form, \nparticularly west of I-95. Daytime temperatures could struggle to \neven reach into the high 50s this weekend, noticeably cool for Mid-\nOctober high temperatures. For added context, the mountains \ntypically have their first freeze October 11, Shenandoah Valley area \nOctober 21, and east of the Blue Ridge November 1.\n\nTo start off next week, high pressure builds and remains overhead \nthrough next Wednesday. A dry and benign weather pattern is expected \nduring this time frame. Next week, daytime temperature gradually \nwarm, returning to near normal fall conditions.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /07Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nA cold front over the region today is going to produce widespread \nclouds with passing showers. MVFR then eventually IFR CIGs develop \nfrom west to east, with CHO beginning the earliest. The IFR CIGs are \nmost likely this evening into tonight, then CIGs improve and VFR \nconditions return after sunrise Friday.\n\nA strong cold front crosses the area Friday afternoon, then a \nreinforcing cold front moves through Saturday morning. A line of \nshowers and thunderstorm along the front could bring brief sub-VFR \nconditions and strong wind gusts. Overrunning showers and low clouds \nare possible Friday night into Saturday morning, then conditions \nquickly return to VFR after sunrise Saturday. \n\nStrong northwest winds behind the front could gust up to 25-30 knots \nSaturday afternoon. Winds quickly diminish Saturday evening as high \npressure builds overhead.\n\nWinds quickly weaken by Sunday remaining light through the beginning \nof next week. VFR conditions are expected for all terminals during \nthis period with variable winds on Sunday become predominantly \nsoutherly by Monday. \n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nA brief surge of north to northeast winds expected through this \nafternoon, with winds around 10-15 knots. Some gusts around 20 knots \nare possible through this afternoon. Winds diminish this evening and \nturn south. \n\nSoutherly channeling likely produces SCA conditions across all the \nwaters by early Friday afternoon. A line of showers and \nthunderstorms is likely to move across the waters Friday evening. \nSpecial Marine Warnings may be needed for thunderstorms capable of \nproducing wind gusts over 35-40 knots.\n\nWinds turn northwest behind a strong cold front Friday night into \nSaturday. Gale conditions are possible during this time, especially \nover the open waters of the Chesapeake Bay. Regardless, hazardous \nmarine conditions with peak gusts around 25-30 knots. Winds start to \nslowly diminish Saturday night, but still gust around 20-25\nknots.\n\nSCA-level wind conditions could linger Sunday morning before \nsubsiding below SCA criteria by Sunday evening and into next week. \nNortheasterly winds on Sunday shift east to southerly on Monday.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...KRR/KVH\nAVIATION...KRR/KVH\nMARINE...KRR/KVH\n"},"forecast":{"t":1792063573,"days":{"2026-10-15":{"hi":64,"lo":59,"pop":76,"short":"Rain Showers"},"2026-10-16":{"hi":83,"lo":51,"pop":73,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-17":{"hi":55,"lo":40,"pop":56,"short":"Rain Showers Likely then Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-18":{"hi":57,"lo":42,"pop":15,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-19":{"hi":62,"lo":46,"pop":14,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-20":{"hi":66,"lo":48,"pop":11,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-21":{"hi":66,"lo":48,"pop":4,"short":"Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1792062000,"n":48,"ceil":[5600,5600,5400,5700,5400,5300,5700,5600,5200,4500,3700,3000,2600,2100,1800,1600,1400,1100,800,500,400,400,300,300,400,400,1200,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,6.0,6.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,8.1,7.0,7.0,7.0,7.0,7.0,7.0,7.0,7.0,7.0,7.7,9.8,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,5.0,5.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0],"spd":[8,7,7,7,7,6,6,5,5,5,4,5,5,5,4,4,4,4,3,3,4,4,4,5,6,7,8,8,9,9,9,9,10,10,9,9,9,9,8,8,7,7,7,8,9,10,11,12],"gst":[15,14,13,12,11,10,9,8,7,7,8,8,9,10,10,10,10,10,9,9,9,10,10,11,12,13,14,15,15,15,15,15,16,16,16,17,17,17,17,17,16,16,17,17,18,18,19,20],"dir":[40,40,50,50,50,50,60,60,60,70,90,90,100,110,120,120,120,120,130,140,150,160,180,190,190,200,200,210,210,210,210,200,210